10:22:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Because “heat” affects fabric differently depending on whether it’s combined with **water, time, and movement**. - **Hot washing** soaks the fibers, heats them for a long time, and tumbles/agitated them. Water lets many natural fibers—especially wool, cotton, rayon, and linen—relax, swell, or rearrange. In wool, agitation plus heat and moisture can make the tiny fiber scales lock together (“felting”), causing permanent shrinkage. - **Ironing** is usually brief and localized. Even if the iron’s plate is hotter than the wash water, it touches the fabric for only seconds, and its heat is quickly absorbed and spread out. You also generally aren’t stretching, tumbling, or rubbing the garment the way a washer does. - **Dry versus wet heat matters.** Some fibers are much more likely to shrink or deform when hot *and wet*. An iron may use steam, but it is usually not soaking the whole garment for a long period. That said, an iron can absolutely ruin fabric if it’s too hot: it can scorch cotton/linen, make wool shiny or felted, and melt or distort synthetics like polyester, nylon, and acrylic. Care labels’ ironing temperatures are based on what the fiber can tolerate for short contact—not what it can safely endure while wet, agitated, and heated for an entire wash cycle.
