10:23:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your grandma’s advice is basically right: **a hot iron can shrink or damage wool**, even without washing it. The simple version is: - Wool fibers are sensitive to heat. - If wool gets too hot—especially with steam or any dampness—the fibers can tighten, flatten, or felt, making the sweater smaller, denser, misshapen, or shiny. - A hot iron also presses the knit structure down, so it may look and feel “shrunk” even if the individual fibers have not literally melted or contracted much. The reason wool-care advice says to use a **low setting, steam carefully, and a pressing cloth**, or to hover the iron rather than press directly, is exactly to avoid that. The only nuance is that a properly used iron can sometimes be set hotter than the wash water because it touches the garment briefly. But that does **not** mean wool is safe at high iron temperatures. A hot iron left on wool, pressed hard into it, or used with steam can definitely ruin it.
